# compute/ — peer-executed CI stage a2o bindings

Two feature files. `peer-executed-stage.feature` exercises the same delegated-compute
substrate `held/features/dataplane/delegated-sweettest.feature` measures (held until `local-conductor` is available), shaped as a CI stage (§3 D1-D2
of the design doc) rather than a Holochain feedback sweettest — the `feedback_signal::`
namespace token in the envelope's `expectedTests` is a pinned-executor requirement, not a
claim about what kind of test this is (see design §3 D6). `measure-on-a-peer.feature` proves
the developer-facing side of the same substrate: the verb that routes a measure-class run to
a neighbour's peer rather than the developer's own desk, born red alongside the habit
`measure-runs-on-a-peer` (genesis/agentic/.epr-meta/measure-runs-on-a-peer.habit.md).

**Considered, no enforcing rule beyond the parent's.** This manifest exists to satisfy the
parent `genesis/a2o/.epr-meta` no-orphan-subtree rule (`new-feature-subdir-needs-meta`) and
to claim coverage (`covers: subtree`) so the coverage walk terminates here, following the
precedent of `genesis/a2o/features/dataplane/resiliency-saga/.epr-meta`. The real authoring
discipline (tag requirements, RED-FIRST assertions, step-definition placement) is the
parent `CLAUDE.md`/`README.md`'s prose convention; no new drift has been observed in this
tree that a rule would catch better than review.
